# Source pin for pip-audit, the vulnerability scanner run over this repo's
# requirements locks by .github/workflows/dependency-audit.yml (issue #804).
# This file lists only the top-level tool; the fully resolved, hash-pinned lock
# that pip actually installs is scripts/ci/requirements-audit.txt (generated
# from this file).
#
# pip-audit gets its own lock rather than joining scripts/lint/requirements-lint.in
# because that lock is installed on every developer session by
# .claude/hooks/session-start.sh and in four separate CI job steps, none of which
# need pip-audit's fairly large closure (cyclonedx-python-lib, rich, requests,
# CacheControl, ...). One CI tool, one lock, matching requirements-semgrep.in.
#
# The lock generated from this file is itself audited by
# scripts/ci/audit_requirements.py, which discovers every scripts/**/requirements*.txt,
# so the scanner's own dependencies are covered by the same gate as everything else.
#
# Regenerating the lock (do this whenever the pin here changes):
#
#   uv pip compile --universal --python-version 3.10 --generate-hashes \
#       scripts/ci/requirements-audit.in -o scripts/ci/requirements-audit.txt
#
# That command keeps the versions already present in the lock, so on its own it
# only re-resolves what a changed pin in this file forces. Transitive releases
# published since the last run are left alone, including security fixes for
# packages nothing here pins directly (issue #788). Add --upgrade to re-resolve
# the whole closure, or -P/--upgrade-package NAME to move one package and
# whatever it drags with it, which keeps a security bump to a reviewable diff.
#
# --universal + --python-version 3.10 make the lock a superset covering every
# interpreter the install site uses (setup-python's "3.x" in the workflow), so
# every marker-gated and platform-gated entry resolves under --require-hashes.
pip-audit==2.10.1
